Revocation is only useful if all credentials stop working. Otherwise an agent can remain operational after the registry says it is suspended.

**Competitor benchmark:**
Microsoft Agent 365 revocation/quarantine and AWS AgentCore Identity credential lifecycle.

**Recommended remediation:**
1. Introduce credential cascade policies per lifecycle transition.
2. Recheck agent lifecycle during credential verification.
3. Add background revocation jobs for long-running sessions and leases.
4. Add audit events linking lifecycle transition to credential revocation.

**Acceptance criteria:**
1. Suspended, quarantined, revoked, and decommissioned agents cannot authenticate with previously valid credentials.
2. Credential status changes are persisted and auditable.
3. Runtime sessions and Tool Gateway calls fail closed after agent status changes.

**Suggested tests:**
1. End-to-end test: create credential, suspend agent, verify token rejected.
2. Audit test for lifecycle-to-credential revocation chain.
3. Runtime session rejection test after decommission.

**Risk if ignored:**
Incident response and access control reports will be misleading.

**Remediation complexity:** Medium

